\section{Data}
Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. The series of maximum
daily rainfall of the \py{station["name"]} is analysed
(code \py{station["code"]}, at \py{station["altitude"]}~m above sea level), which
covers \py{n_years}~years, from \py{series["year"].min()} to \py{series["year"].max()}.

The mean of the series is \py{f"{mean:.1f}"}~mm and its standard deviation
\py{f"{std:.1f}"}~mm. The series \pyif{trend > 0.2}{shows a rising trend
of \py{f"{trend:.2f}"}~mm a year}{shows no noticeable trend} over the period.

\section{Gumbel fit}
The method of moments gives a scale $\alpha =
\py{f"{alpha:.2f}"}$~mm and a location $u = \py{f"{u:.2f}"}$~mm. Table~\ref{tab:periods}
is written by the notebook's function \texttt{period\_rows}, called from here with the
periods of interest.

\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \caption{Maximum daily rainfall by return period.}
  \label{tab:periods}
  \begin{tabular}{rrr}
    \toprule
    $T$ (years) & Annual probability (\%) & $P_d$ (mm) \\
    \midrule
    \py{period_rows([2, 5, 10, 25, 50, 100, 500])}
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[H]
